  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Host and Rohingya Enhancement of Lives Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The summary description of the proposed operation is as follows:Component 1: Resilient Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene (WASH). This component will be Implemented by DPHE. The activities include (1a) construction of additional mini piped water supply schemes and point source water options in Host Communities, resulting in minor capacity improvement for water supply and distribution; (1b) rehabilitation of existing water supply and distribution facilities and infrastructure (i.e., mini piped water supply schemes and point source water options); and (1c) O&amp;amp;M of new (from HELP) and existing (from EMCRP) facilities and infrastructure, in DRP camps in Cox&apos;s Bazar and Bhasan Char, until the closing of the project. The component will also support (1d) construction of new household twin pit toilets and public toilets in Host Communities, slightly expanding the current overall capacity in the system, (1e) rehabilitation and improvement of existing household toilets and community latrines (including handwashing facilities in the toilets) in DRP camps in Cox&apos;s Bazar and Bhasan Char, (1f) construction of integrated waste management facilities &#x2013; which could include solid waste management facilities and fecal sludge treatment plants &#x2013; in the Host Communities and in DRP camps in Cox&apos;s Bazar and Bhasan Char, including the necessary equipment and system for collection and transport of waste in the camps; (1g) additional supply of general hygiene kits and menstrual health and hygiene (MHH) kits for women; and (1h) O&amp;amp;M of selected sanitation and hygiene facilities (toilets, latrines, and integrated waste management facilities) constructed under EMCRP and HELP, in DRP camps in Cox&apos;s Bazar and Bhasan Char.Component 2: Climate-and Disaster-Resilient Infrastructure, Energy, and Emergency Response Subcomponent 2.1: Rural infrastructure development: The activities implemented by the Local Government Engineering Department (LGED), will finance interventions on rural infrastructure and facilities under LGED&apos;s jurisdiction, including: (2.1a) construction of multipurpose disaster shelters and expanding the capacity of existing disaster shelters cum primary schools by extending one floor; (2.1b) rehabilitation of existing rural roads with minor enhancement of capacity that are under LGED&apos;s jurisdiction, including associated protective measures such as roadside drainages and slope stabilization measures; (2.1c) rehabilitation and re-construction of existing bridges with minor capacity expansion  under LGED&apos;s jurisdiction;  (2.1d) rehabilitation  of landing stages in Hatiya upazila (for Host Communities) and in Bhasan Char (for the DRP); (2.1e) supply and installation of lightning protection system and solar streetlights; (2.1f) improvement of rural markets (hat-bazar) including repair and rehabilitation of their roadside drainage network; (2.1g) construction and operation of cold storage in Hatiya upazila (for Host Communities) and in Bashan Char (for the DRP); (2.1h) construction, supply and installation of an Emergency Operation Center (EOC) with structural facility for strategic coordination and decision-making capacities during natural disasters, extreme climate events and emergencies; (2.1i) supply of fire safety and search and rescue equipment; (2.1j) small scale afforestation activities in Cox&apos;s Bazar and Bhasan Char; and (2.1k)O&amp;amp;M of existing basic infrastructure in DRP camps in Cox&apos;s Bazar and Bhasan Char. Subcomponent 2.2: Rural roads improvement: The activities implemented by the Roads and Highways Department (RHD), will finance: (2.2a) rehabilitation of existing rural roads under RHD&apos;s jurisdiction ; (2.2b) rehabilitation and re-construction of existing bridges with minor capacity expansion under RHD&apos;s jurisdiction;  and (2.2c) installation of the associated road accessories and protective works, including rehabilitation and improvement of associated roadside drainage and slope stabilization measures. Typical roadside drainages constructed in RHD&apos;s rural roads include RCC saucer drains, RCC cross drains, and RCC U-drains. Concrete slope protection and RCC retaining walls (4m and 7m height) are envisioned for slope stabilization. Roadside accessories to improve safety and future maintenance include, inter alia, traffic signs, signposts, chainage/kilometers posts, road marking, and convex mirrors. The interventions will take place both in Host Communities and in areas outside Cox&apos;s Bazar DRP camps, both improving the region&apos;s connectivity in normal conditions and better improving evacuation and emergency response during extreme weather events.Subcomponent 2.3: Renewable energy: The activities implemented by the Bangladesh Power Development Board (BPDB), will finance the construction of a solar power plant in Bhasan Char DRP camps, using battery energy storage systems (BESS) backed up by a diesel generator. The construction package will comprise the solar power plant along with its associated supporting instruments. Component 3: Strengthening Institutional Systems to Enhance Service Provision and Support Integrated Resilient Development Subcomponent 3.1: GoB capacity building and technical assistance This subcomponent will finance (3.1a) agency-specific capacity building activities, (3.2b) technical assistance and analytical studies, and (3.3c) a comprehensive environmental impact and damage assessment. Subcomponent 3.2: Host communities and DRP awareness raising and skills building program: This subcomponent will support awareness raising and skills building programs for the host communities and DRP, focusing on WASH and disaster risk management and emergency response. Activities aim to support sustainability of interventions at the community and individual levels, complementing institutional strengthening work at the GoB level.  Subcomponent 3.3: Reinforcing the Emergency Management Capacity: This subcomponent will finance the training of first responders and the Fire Service and Civil Defence (FSCD), so that they are better prepared to respond to emergencies and natural disasters.Subcomponent 3.4: Technology-driven M&amp;amp;E and project management : This subcomponent will finance (3.4a) development of a digitized construction monitoring system and (3.4b) project and program management. Activity (3.4a) will support the modernization of implementing agencies systems in remote supervision through the procurement and skills building for cutting-edge technologies. Activity (3.4b) will include day-to-day administration, management, monitoring, and coordination of project activities (including Financial Management (FM), E&amp;amp;S, procurement, M&amp;amp;E, and reporting).Component 4: Contingent Emergency Response: This will ensure provision of immediate response to an eligible crisis. In such an event, the component will contribute to immediate and effective response. In the event of an emergency balance under the other components can be reallocated to the Contingency Emergency Response Component, in the event of an emergency. Funds from the WHR can only be used for support to Host Communities and DRP.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To improve access to basic services and enhance disaster and climate resilience of the Host Communities and Displaced Rohingya Population (DRP).</narrative>
  </description>
